<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muawiya I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/661">661</a>–<a href="/years/680">680 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, Muawiya, Muawiyah, Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muawiya I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, ruling from 661 to 680. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali in the first Muslim civil war, and after Ali's death he became caliph, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and established hereditary succession by nominating his son Yazid.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muawiya-i.jpg" alt="Muawiya I" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LouisAragon (uploader) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab-Sasanian_coin_of_Muawiyah_I,_struck_at_the_Fasa_mint_in_Darabjird_(Fars).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Abi Talib’s troubled caliphate from 656 to 661 CE, dominated by the First Fitna, the civil war between Ali and the governor of Syria, <b>Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan</b>, over responsibility for Uthman’s murder, showed that the caliphate lacked mechanisms for resolving elite political conflict without resort to arms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Abi Talib, the fourth of the Rashidun caliphs and the cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet Muhammad, when <b>Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan</b>, the long-serving governor of Syria and the most powerful figure in the Islamic world, secured recognition as caliph and made Damascus the seat of the caliphate…</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At Siffin on the Euphrates, Caliph Ali ibn Abi Talib fought <b>Muawiya</b>, governor of Syria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As Arab armies overran the Sasanian lands, the Armenian commander Theodore Rshtuni made a treaty with <b>Muawiya</b> in 653 accepting Arab overlordship in return for autonomy and keeping Armenian troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-armenia">Principality of Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
